WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.898
Stage two: the morning check.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:11.998
Tuesday, six thirty. On Camille's plan: a threshold session, five times six minutes.

3
00:00:12.098 --> 00:00:13.980
But the night was short.

4
00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:24.536
Before any decision, the coach reads three signals, always together: overnight heart rate variability, resting heart rate, and readiness.

5
00:00:24.636 --> 00:00:30.766
Three nights below the personal band, six beats above normal, readiness at thirty-four.

6
00:00:31.616 --> 00:00:33.738
Verdict: ease off.

7
00:00:33.838 --> 00:00:43.496
Threshold waits for the signals to recover. Tonight, forty-five minutes of easy running, between six and seven: the coolest slot.

8
00:00:44.346 --> 00:00:48.172
The decision is written to a file, with its sources.

9
00:00:48.272 --> 00:00:51.282
It shows up on the dashboard, under “Why today?”.

10
00:00:52.346 --> 00:00:58.212
And if Camille asks why, the /why command answers by quoting the measurements.

11
00:00:58.312 --> 00:01:00.434
Never by making things up.

12
00:01:01.284 --> 00:01:06.814
Too much data? The check is configurable: full, minimal, or off.

13
00:01:06.914 --> 00:01:11.364
And the coach never quietly switches a stricter level back on.

14
00:01:13.914 --> 00:01:17.692
To go further: the “Today” page of the documentation.
